 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The server delivers committed changes through self-hosted WebSocket sessions, avoiding a realtime-provider dependency while providing low-latency updates.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around WebSocket delivery. The change store retains replayable event revisions and reliably publishes committed changes. Delivery manages socket capacity, session lifecycle, and publication. Browsers reconnect after a disconnect and catch up from their last received revision. The team accepts the connection and reconnect operations work in exchange for fast updates without a recurring realtime vendor cost or coupling.
 
